Wolt Web Application Security Assessment
1. Executive Summary
This assessment focused on reviewing the security of the Wolt web application through application mapping and targeted security testing.
The assessment covered authentication, authorization, API behavior, object access, sensitive data exposure, and HTTP header manipulation.
No security vulnerabilities were identified during the documented testing.


2. Scope
Testing was performed against the Wolt web application and the functionality available within the authorized testing scope.
The assessment focused on:
Customer functionality
Authentication
User/account functionality
Shopping functionality
API endpoints identified during mapping
Relevant application workflows
Testing was performed using authorized test accounts and within the restrictions defined by the program.



3. Methodology
The assessment followed this methodology:
Application
↓
Users
↓
Capabilities
↓
Business Objects
↓
Application Workflow
↓
Endpoint Mapping
↓
Security Testing
↓
Findings
For each relevant capability, the associated business object and application flow were identified before testing the underlying functionality.


4. Application Overview
Wolt is a delivery platform connecting:
Customers
Restaurants / merchants
Courier partners
The customer application allows users to browse stores and products, search for products, manage a shopping cart, manage their profile, and interact with delivery-related functionality.


5. Features Tested
The following functionality was mapped and tested:
Authentication
Login flow
Authentication tokens
Unauthenticated requests
Invalid authentication tokens
Customer Account
User profile
/v1/user/me endpoint
Authentication enforcement
Sensitive data exposure
Application Access Control
Object-level access
IDOR testing where applicable
Authorization behavior
HTTP Request Testing
Authorization header manipulation
Origin header manipulation
Referer header manipulation

6. Findings
No vulnerabilities identified
The tested functionality correctly enforced authentication and rejected invalid authentication credentials.
No exploitable IDOR, sensitive data exposure, or authentication bypass was identified during the assessment.
Header manipulation testing also did not identify a security vulnerability.


7. Recommendation
Although no vulnerabilities were identified during this assessment, the application should continue to enforce:
Strong authentication controls
Server-side authorization
Object-level access controls
Proper validation of authentication tokens
Protection of sensitive user information
Regular security testing should also be performed as application functionality changes.


8. Conclusion
The assessment demonstrated a structured approach to application security testing by first mapping the application's users, capabilities, business objects, workflows, and API endpoints before performing targeted security tests.
No vulnerabilities were identified within the tested functionality.


